ill obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c8bceae-a8f8-59df-9913-53898f44b673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485052} :answer-field/id #uuid "16cf85a0-d4e0-503b-a40c-377068739a10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df658338-ffbc-54a4-8fc3-4233fa4b9c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485286} :answer-field/id #uuid "91a5a09a-06e9-5e46-b927-635849a5367f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485429} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3e847ec-d131-5985-94dc-25f91ed6df64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485336} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2e3df9-3029-583e-b5fd-63d9b4b790dc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485478}] :question/id #uuid "c4c5958e-629a-595b-ba26-53d736ebe0b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-320017" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$ gives a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which would require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$ gives a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which requires finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f10f67-df03-50c7-a1bf-5e006341a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549232} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a1b35ca-c7e1-59ae-a856-f56012ef0b81" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c772d00-f765-5d8f-bc2e-32016f3f2865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549237} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48b1de5-1483-57d3-9d6b-7c2d15925062" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549243}] :question/id #uuid "06d6225d-4b01-5c48-bc90-403dc6edd027" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 96\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ece529b6-c6ae-5615-90bc-4d54796df05b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549244} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd9b3b77-a05b-5a30-b798-0de9e3ac89a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549241} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8f4cd3d-eb75-5365-9f9a-85d1850ed7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549240} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce2d2cb8-9afc-5890-a68a-634867abbb6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549242}] :question/id #uuid "8ccaf12f-b22e-5cd6-9d78-52cfd8d406ea" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-110 & -110\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c3e2b8e-8c83-5ad9-92ba-e5bf2691d00f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549233} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eddf070-87e3-5dd1-9c6c-8c102e5b2871" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549234} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6ea8b1-6eae-5fef-aee9-bc00462cb7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549235} :answer-field/id #uuid "86fc0d6b-ed48-5943-b4fd-deee378fe8ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f02708cf-b1b9-5464-8710-caa8ec92dce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549245} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ec97ff-39a5-521f-ac19-40acb2e2b077" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549247}] :question/id #uuid "f78f9635-d6b7-5289-960e-001285f39f9d" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$ABC=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $(AB)$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)\\,C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & 0 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115058 :question/id #uuid "7ab158e6-4222-5cab-88a0-85ce74a7d4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4413" :question/problem "Given that $A, B,$ and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n\nII. $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n\nIII. $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\nAB + AC  \\neq BA + CA.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545182 :question-generator/id #uuid "227b6b15-7345-547c-a312-6f2320ca1a08" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186115228.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1067aa3d-2b51-5b1a-b638-8cd254e921c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448632} {:answer/id #uuid "a3967aa3-4b50-5c5c-b5fe-aa477f5fe032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448632} :answer-field/id #uuid "149d811f-1bfd-59da-848a-31d1c4100def" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86075d40-0e3f-586b-9449-798158d86bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448634} {:answer/id #uuid "1fec6718-135d-53ed-ace0-3373f23e193b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448635} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdc4938-08ac-5768-9c3a-6cf1b96bf157" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8546691d-f8b4-5754-96f8-903691eb5eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448636} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f078ae-15b0-55b8-85f1-fb559cdbab88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448637} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc3e7d1-fdc0-5ebf-9944-36f12a7d8eff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449358}] :question/id #uuid "5e648555-87e2-52bf-ac70-24ae7f7d32c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42265" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n- **II.** $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- **III.** $AC+BC=(A+B)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84adee91-f9b3-5089-890e-41b5e257813d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448638} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e31c4f-b7bd-5276-a991-deb172fd1702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448639} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1bd3d1-8fdb-5689-a908-f1bc0ac9ac01" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1670a685-9e94-5937-8cfd-7d0d29af6eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448640} {:answer/id #uuid "16ec1bf8-e36a-5bfb-84bd-a4334c07e464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448640} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b55508-2a98-5d52-9e53-d6536bd357ec" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449360} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "113b4e99-4e5b-55aa-a4da-2a14a868af02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448642} {:answer/id #uuid "056450c3-2bbe-583e-950a-5fcae4730b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448642} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d5b3ee-a8ea-534b-8e66-6ca762d54d43" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449361}] :question/id #uuid "174631dd-f119-50e8-bf7b-ac669b330d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-42266" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+B)C=AC+BC$\n- **II.** $AB=BA$\n- **III.** $A(BC)=(BA)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5721fddb-4ddb-5ceb-8d53-beb5879aabb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448386} {:answer/id #uuid "94b7a979-8ec2-54e2-90a3-0c1ddb63d731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448387} :answer-field/id #uuid "808c3874-e879-50ff-9b4f-7080ca2b02ad" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449233} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f74fb5a-177b-5835-b20e-ce0567027248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448388} {:answer/id #uuid "06d94795-ebf6-5369-a12b-dbfa3c0bde79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448388} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bc69198-ea95-5680-86fb-59feb160bb36" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449234} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f629e2cf-0d6b-5ebd-ba20-35e8b11008d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448390} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab0f40e-90eb-52f5-8bf5-16588c5aca46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448390} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7d9849-da3b-534a-83ba-7cddee8c3884" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449235}] :question/id #uuid "8250ce52-a1e9-536b-9512-ee968aac3cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-130446" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${A}^{2}B=A(AB)$\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- **III.** $B(CA)=(CB)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  ${A}^{2}B=AAB=A(AB)$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false.  Matrix multiplication is distributive and  $A(B-C)=AB-AC.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AC\\ne CA$ in general and therefore $A(B-C)\\ne AB-CA$, in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $B(CA)=(BC)A.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general and therefore $B(CA)\\ne (CB)A$, in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420d6cc9-8195-5ce4-9357-adff9f06e789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449472} {:answer/id #uuid "dfaff5dd-3001-57cc-a214-27e4b2f13a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449473} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83edd0f-1ee5-57a0-b8f2-861740202905" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a96d375-e3c1-5d42-b10d-88fedd420645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449474} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d1552b-b0c1-5914-8616-b9aa0f71d996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449474} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5d71a8-1832-50ab-a267-82a5ea4c6510" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fd4a080-8694-528b-99c0-94bb7afe0160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449476} {:answer/id #uuid "b70ca1e9-55e1-5e83-8b86-ac7385727b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449477} :answer-field/id #uuid "43485488-f54f-56dd-98bd-bbf9a37fb3ae" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450692}] :question/id #uuid "9a216ad8-b070-56d6-a23a-d32e7322b41f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130449" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $3\\times 3$ matrices, and  ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=A{I}_{3}B$\n- **II.** $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB$\n- **III.** ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $A{I}_{3}B=(A{I}_{3})B=AB.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB+A{I}_{3}=AB+A.$ However,  $AB+A\\ne AB,$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7957644-0d34-5355-b4aa-6843421f4cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449478} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfd816a-e282-554a-bc11-458d1326d8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449479} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fcf8fbd-79c9-5a3f-9c93-3269eb1a6b18" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5609b0ad-b55f-5942-bbdd-8d585ee962ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449480} {:answer/id #uuid "8db8304a-b1d0-5052-b18c-e9bb08899b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449480} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b9348d-3f50-5361-9ba1-8262be95c0a0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5099a79-f035-55b7-a74f-976fa8b79237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449482} {:answer/id #uuid "96ca6167-ad12-543c-a1c9-e7474101a124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449482} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd08e90-18cc-5f16-8ac5-f89ed5e9b611" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450695}] :question/id #uuid "da216097-76ab-5a2e-b3ad-662534506edb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130455" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(BC)=(AB)C$\n\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n\n- **III.** $CA=AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III  is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $CA\\ne AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3e23a78-9cff-5f4f-b321-68256103a287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449484} {:answer/id #uuid "fc24f783-305e-584a-96dc-56e90c7199d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449485} :answer-field/id #uuid "599aa420-34d9-5e48-96d9-1fff3725e551" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd393f3-55f2-599a-aa0a-d18ad1ef34f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449486} {:answer/id #uuid "1566549e-0426-589d-92ea-3d623677f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449486} :answer-field/id #uuid "d184eea5-c9c5-5b93-8586-c6e75fec6d77" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7fff099-59fc-5bf2-850f-508b365824eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449488} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b157f5-5880-5d42-b0af-3314832fb23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449489} :answer-field/id #uuid "c228a2f4-4fa2-5cb5-ad55-1810d368d693" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450698}] :question/id #uuid "c886d4f7-2a2b-5730-a24b-3976b9e31d35" :question/math-academy-id "q-130463" :question/problem "Given that $A$ and $B$  are $8\\times 8$ matrices,  and ${I}_{8}$ is the $8\\times 8$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A{I}_{8}={I}_{8}A$\n- **II.** $AB=BA$\n- **III.** $A\\cdot 2{I}_{8}=2A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{8}={I}_{8}A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot 2{I}_{8} & =A({I}_{8}+{I}_{8}) \\\\  & =A{I}_{8}+A{I}_{8} \\\\  & =A+A \\\\  & =2A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I  and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f505436-de5e-5e74-9c16-db47f9d3dde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449490} {:answer/id #uuid "55ad8fd2-983a-52f3-adb4-1610dbb1063d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449490} :answer-field/id #uuid "61886434-6b69-5a05-bc4e-5fe067d4aadb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450699} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e4cb8a-161c-5b37-8171-e110c0ccabdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449492} {:answer/id #uuid "c53c0d38-c0d6-534c-98d5-3e8b2cf7c4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449493} :answer-field/id #uuid "6517376b-dc67-5612-af55-6316cd92e350" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45243252-dbf8-5dba-81eb-702f3231cb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449494} {:answer/id #uuid "5489dffc-c13b-505b-b7ba-aa63f4669cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449494} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd0867ba-7318-50a7-adfb-0f8ddb112341" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450701}] :question/id #uuid "ca328f5c-fde3-5a0e-88ed-9bc8ff6b0334" :question/math-academy-id "q-130478" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(CA)B=C(BA)$\n- **II.** $(A+A)B=2AB$\n- **III.** $CB=BC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $(CA)B=C(AB)$ is always true. However,  matrix multiplication is not commutative,   $AB\\ne BA$ in general. Therefore, $(CA)B\\ne C(BA)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+A)B=AB+AB=2AB$ is always true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, so $CB\\ne BC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d094e2-1177-5e99-96ad-7caeee897640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450618} {:answer/id #uuid "ca99c815-da6d-51d7-a07d-611d7659f788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450618} :answer-field/id #uuid "c30fd7ce-bd14-59fb-ab10-845f8a59bb92" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956b7a25-6517-5ef2-837c-39c50a5c5a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450620} {:answer/id #uuid "bd63bdd4-4b06-56ad-b67b-a613cc1dd46f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450621} :answer-field/id #uuid "382ec9ba-1720-5328-8c38-f194ab93dcab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "762ad639-2afc-55e3-bdc7-be4e7057c714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450622} {:answer/id #uuid "7a78eef3-ff9c-56b5-98cb-545a4b324d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450622} :answer-field/id #uuid "800a3551-29f1-5bb8-a3bc-66ca201c01c9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451265}] :question/id #uuid "005a0156-3d99-59d1-ab99-adf9b9b4c7b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-42264" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n\n- **II.** $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n\n- **III.** $A(B-C)=(B-C)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $A(B-C)\\ne (B-C)A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ede5fc7-4e98-54a5-a8b6-e3d24c4c38db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450624} {:answer/id #uuid "55e4960c-b4a8-5dc4-9358-5474cf82f7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450624} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5404b0-68d0-5679-9acd-82c4cfdc5828" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451266} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7725433-91f4-58a8-86f9-aeb838dcca83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450626} {:answer/id #uuid "db204d99-d48d-5b15-a94d-fd45955b1ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450627} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d78dc9-a409-525c-846b-1acf74456b6c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6be3f3c0-0d5f-53d8-b5d4-1cdbc4330e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450628} {:answer/id #uuid "32dd403d-28b4-5b42-93d6-1c1a8a1630d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450629} :answer-field/id #uuid "baab8b40-76af-5c21-b0d3-f31812f8c1dd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451268}] :question/id #uuid "cea2f24d-948b-5d70-91c6-23df57186795" :question/math-academy-id "q-42267" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $5\\times 5$ matrices, and  ${I}_{5}$ is the $5\\times 5$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n- **II.** $A(B+C)=AB+AC$\n- **III.** $B{I}_{5}={I}_{5}B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $B{I}_{5}={I}_{5}B=B.$\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5b4b6c9-b120-5669-8778-9d44bd03a705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451627} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ff174-54f1-5fc0-82c9-56c7f68be83b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451628} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6596886-eb35-5a15-8678-0d03898ac57c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452661} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b1b7dcd-0a45-5a31-b1e7-359ba6b8ce48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451629} {:answer/id #uuid "2844641e-66b1-56cc-8371-e36f6a1b3ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451629} :answer-field/id #uuid "648f05bf-a002-5add-8c9a-e67860f117ba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aec13ecc-22bc-5436-b215-dddd0d181f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451631} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfc103f-6039-5ed8-b224-6f15716dcaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451631} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce1dbe9-397b-565f-a4c9-e832cb32d032" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452663}] :question/id #uuid "1c88851c-8567-55d2-897e-8b0f9b020c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-75662" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(B+C)=AC+AB$\n- **II.** $A(BC)=(BA)C$\n- **III.** $AB=BA$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd2d4af-fcc8-55b6-bf9b-9feed35a4e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451633} {:answer/id #uuid "8c44c3d1-8408-5b27-9d56-5820850d8e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451633} :answer-field/id #uuid "875098b9-5492-5e1a-b53e-55972f8d78c0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c09b308-44c0-5d82-b117-e79d490fc908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451635} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea9fe4-7d06-5ee2-81e0-6e343876dfb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451636} :answer-field/id #uuid "f365105a-7265-5164-bb84-ae009541d7d4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086cedd9-2c5d-5cf5-8342-fff602804217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451637} {:answer/id #uuid "d576ec60-2dbd-5562-90ac-ff700e520b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451638} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf272d3-96d9-5e52-bf3a-2077d76af75c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452666}] :question/id #uuid "8619c3de-3523-5f87-a248-dac65df502b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-75664" :question/problem "Given that $A$ and $B$  are $4\\times 4$ matrices,  and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n- **II.** $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n- **III.** $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A{I}_{4}=AB+A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d29adab0-49f5-5be8-9dfb-41b652b9f78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451639} {:answer/id #uuid "aae53705-b1e7-5ad4-a458-fc12bd5715e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451639} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec2f6a3-b7c4-5dbf-b313-e1460364d497" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163312b5-47ae-51a4-8302-780627f27eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451641} {:answer/id #uuid "8391eb9d-de48-5257-958c-71e626ee3926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451641} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e9e4ab6-972e-5a28-8a06-204e593f15c3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452668} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5abcda23-f4db-5653-8749-2127e4eb2cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451643} {:answer/id #uuid "83789ace-8be4-5fa7-bb78-667b324a5f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451644} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebdef35b-4dc7-5168-954f-85f588629353" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452669}] :question/id #uuid "8ca7b9cd-e052-55a9-b9b9-9f438a40909d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75717" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $CB=BC$\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- **III.** $A(BC)=(AB)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\nTherefore, only statement  III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0baea8e6-67f2-5e1f-8d1f-49751d15a460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484839} {:answer/id #uuid "150d4133-0a25-50d9-a5b6-3426cedc7ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484857} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bd451c-1d47-509f-8a91-0bb7d9a2e039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485211} {:answer/id #uuid "b779c7a3-5d4b-56ea-9ee5-63a3e5e72954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485212} {:answer/id #uuid "d6de882f-5289-5eb0-83ae-5e40d163532c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484839} :answer-field/id #uuid "72242043-aa0f-565d-a557-78a4b73b318a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485413}] :question/id #uuid "96d290b8-46cc-5702-9916-bb75bce9f305" :question/math-academy-id "q-75663" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n\nII. $(CA)B=C(AB)$\n\nIII. $BC=CB$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is asociative, so $(CA)B=C(AB)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I  and II are true."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120652} :activity/id #uuid "acb4d472-a6fc-5c2c-a30f-1918865acaa4" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fcf3704-4856-5095-a6f4-7ec20d81f3c4"} :step/id #uuid "8cb786bc-aa30-57b8-aa52-e09a7018f11b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d"} :step/id #uuid "0691d00f-77e7-57d5-b71b-b1153b0b4afa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121027 :tutorial/content "**Cramer's Rule** is a method for solving a linear system of equations using determinants.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/c7/c7ef883a1d4dd8c1fc0fc8fbde5ed80c9a5229650fc77c77559b71552370c35b.svg) Then Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\nx =  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{D_y}{D}\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n![](images/b1/b13ecd10395fe0977d04d627179667cbca207fcf4015a89d43df0e69765f1f5e.svg)\n\nFor example, let's use Cramer's rule to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y=-1 \\\\ -1x+2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the **determinant of the coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $D_x$ and $D_y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{4}{4}=1, \\\\ y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{8}{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is common to write the solutions to a linear system as a vector. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y \\right> = \\left< 1,2 \\right>.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3adc5c1c-58e5-5c0a-a8f7-da6abd6f06e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3102 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "48887938-d6a0-5822-b084-3bf10a3a4461" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7580024-f357-57d4-94f2-85c25e2c144b"} :step/id #uuid "720a6f93-380c-5e70-8c81-e99f44a4ef11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120647}}] :activity/title "Cramer's Rule for 2x2 Systems of Linear Equations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119997}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050129 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119599 :question/id #uuid "4efedc4e-19e9-552c-bc27-814df953e141" :question/math-academy-id "e-4575" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y &=-1 \\\\ -3x+3y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nExpress $y$ in terms of $2 \\times 2$ determinants." :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y &=-1 \\\\ -3x+3y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_y$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fcf3704-4856-5095-a6f4-7ec20d81f3c4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6feb8d82-ec98-5ab3-916e-e0964f921e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441130} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa3964d-0f84-58b2-a2df-245a34859a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,2 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 15 & \\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441133} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f9ea97-6403-5269-8dae-1465c6fa454a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,2 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441136} {:answer/id #uuid "e54fae11-f4e6-587e-bf65-bc9a931e3cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441138} {:answer/id #uuid "fefcd620-c9c3-502f-a8a1-81af7c238754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,2 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}|}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,2 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}|}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441136} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a46614-828d-5519-82a8-fe722bf6d03a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118736}] :question/id #uuid "fe230bdd-3a20-59d7-b749-5cc881ab022a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42605" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=2 \\\\ x+5y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=2 \\\\ x+5y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e715af-790d-5a45-940a-a673b6523851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 19\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327569} {:answer/id #uuid "589d00f8-cc64-58e5-bdbd-a55f8aa69781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,3 & 15 \\\\ -2 & -9\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327710} {:answer/id #uuid "adcb6869-0950-5393-9da2-11bbb3a812d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328444} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ec29d0-dac7-53f5-bdf0-8055be291ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328682} {:answer/id #uuid "ff84764b-30f5-589e-b868-b840ae55f410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328682} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5fa56cf-33c9-52dd-be6c-7ca2cd8c7c49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329534}] :question/id #uuid "2c2e31ac-502a-53cd-a44d-a0ec535a4195" :question/math-academy-id "q-42606" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y &=15 \\\\ 2x-3y &=-9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y &=15 \\\\ 2x-3y &=-9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "530f7601-74e9-540a-82ef-db390518a6fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327654} {:answer/id #uuid "79e605a3-e423-5657-86b5-efaa7336486b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}32 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 32 \\\\ -3 & 26\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327978} {:answer/id #uuid "81226cb7-cf78-526b-97c8-cf96fa7e7496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328047} {:answer/id #uuid "964a6cc0-3d53-58e3-a342-0e852fe595d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}32 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 15\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328240} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b4809e-a7e6-57f1-8a82-dee22d55f5e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}32 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}32 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328541} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d31b58d-0925-5521-9b7e-3d57b98648de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329445}] :question/id #uuid "8917d445-979c-51b8-8583-088afcba0337" :question/math-academy-id "q-42607" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+6y &=32 \\\\ 3x+5y &=26.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+6y &=32 \\\\ 3x+5y &=26.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}32 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}32 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0462fc7d-5602-5e11-be7f-5440cc584531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,1\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,-2 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3 & -1\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326979} {:answer/id #uuid "1d080d71-449d-5f4f-9e74-855a1533f0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -11 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,1\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327185} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc38da3-e896-5921-8f14-5ffd9c3514c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -11 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,11\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,-2 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3 & -1\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327465} {:answer/id #uuid "abc469f7-5ab4-5e1b-a7e8-df652f905329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -11 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & -11\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328421} {:answer/id #uuid "c6584bcc-603d-56ef-b88a-6a876c257496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -11 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,1\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,-2 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,11 & -1\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -11 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,11\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,-2 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3 & -1\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327465} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f05ab2-e1d9-5608-a9ec-64c63fef9223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329299}] :question/id #uuid "68908acf-8e7f-5d06-8e61-d1f1d1667a0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42608" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y &=-11 \\\\ 3x-y &=11.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y &=-11 \\\\ 3x-y &=11.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -11 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -11 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fdfaa1-dc80-5816-beee-bbbbfa47d49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,13 & 7 \\\\ 19 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326998} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9784f6-a1b1-5af2-afb6-d2b27ab576cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}13 & \\,\\,\\,4 \\\\ 19 & -2\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327491} {:answer/id #uuid "808a947b-d5c1-5dbf-8491-3da0e4d90f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 13 \\\\ -2 & 19\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328041} {:answer/id #uuid "856a56f5-3160-5afa-b12c-787915460f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,2 & 13 \\\\ -4 & 19\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328083} {:answer/id #uuid "89d94203-e19a-58f0-ae27-e4ad2b9686f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,7 & 13 \\\\ -5 & 19\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 13 \\\\ -2 & 19\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328041} :answer-field/id #uuid "9857b80f-3999-5ca2-8817-c239f942d2d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329438}] :question/id #uuid "6e256198-3231-5478-a0ac-f237d4e7a998" :question/math-academy-id "q-42970" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+7y &=13 \\\\ -2x+5y &=19.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+7y &=13 \\\\ -2x+5y &=19.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 13 \\\\ -2 & 19\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 13 \\\\ -2 & 19\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5724c9ae-dc06-5f04-a7bb-16c79f89623e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}21 & 5 \\\\ 12 & 2\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336831} {:answer/id #uuid "79aa5121-234b-5948-84ff-1f6f15cbba44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ 21 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337126} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ea90dc-0d02-5398-9ad2-067e1cf603ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 21\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337756} {:answer/id #uuid "e5721947-bbab-5d0d-9e98-e795547ab7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 21 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338034} {:answer/id #uuid "f242b257-2ab8-51b4-b202-dfd535c14a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}12 & 2 \\\\ 21 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}12 & 2 \\\\ 21 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338152} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4bf4df1-eabd-54c5-9df4-7454b683ca46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338609}] :question/id #uuid "04beac6b-a557-5165-adec-a531623a11c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-70106" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=12 \\\\ 4x+5y &=21.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=12 \\\\ 4x+5y &=21.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}12 & 2 \\\\ 21 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}12 & 2 \\\\ 21 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81a9c740-e176-5d4f-b580-d90c73c50672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388634} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad108c0-dc3e-5d9f-bd6c-a73cc3fbf1cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388635} {:answer/id #uuid "efc33b0f-b9f4-5215-ae08-da3aa1c28f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 17\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388636} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c4f737-0790-54cf-88d7-2c95fda98258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-14 & 2 \\\\ 13 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388637} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3a1b35-f59f-515b-b7d3-68b5c1ed8ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-14 & 2 \\\\ 13 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388634} :answer-field/id #uuid "46a433c9-a1b6-5e55-9a26-b5a8790a2ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388633}] :question/id #uuid "3dee3a3d-b589-52d5-8b29-2264540bb947" :question/math-academy-id "q-139960" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+8y &=-14 \\\\ 5x+4y &=13.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+8y &=-14 \\\\ 5x+4y &=13.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447eaab9-6da2-5044-99db-4a1a4223532f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393683} {:answer/id #uuid "78a86f8d-aae4-5a4d-95e4-fab946aebfd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393684} {:answer/id #uuid "38a07045-f8ae-5e87-9321-f64593af7a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393685} {:answer/id #uuid "e48114b7-c325-58ff-8779-f2b138d44c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393686} {:answer/id #uuid "19c2ae1c-bf55-56a8-9095-d30b83511f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393685} :answer-field/id #uuid "87423d6e-0ff7-5bbe-8eda-f375b8d4efa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393682}] :question/id #uuid "94153e84-a333-5aec-b75c-49c1a118f48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-77398" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=5 \\\\ x-3y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=5 \\\\ x-3y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2929bd-edc2-5508-beb1-1d9807142bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441125} {:answer/id #uuid "c159eb9f-4f2c-5439-9834-15476295ce88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441135} {:answer/id #uuid "d56c8d4b-ec4f-5319-9ebf-a207cab9ae06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441137} {:answer/id #uuid "f0696591-4c24-54b6-8b14-e466d380abaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441140} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c1f9ea-b9de-5947-a51a-a2f5c3cb34c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441125} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d79a455-f937-543f-8ebe-441cfa187191" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441145}] :question/id #uuid "659100c2-e69a-5015-bd46-ea6499418f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-77396" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=2 \\\\ 2x+3y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=2 \\\\ 2x+3y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c31cbf-e411-5a16-8126-ed9a704efadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-8 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552892} {:answer/id #uuid "1c419977-a9db-50ee-ad18-ae9235af700f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -2\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552919} {:answer/id #uuid "3830ad06-0fa6-5789-b242-ad71e1909f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552980} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdc06c8-14af-5ae9-b49d-c138304f6741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553188} {:answer/id #uuid "cc42aca7-24ec-5837-8d84-2adcce5421a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553277} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b06cb65-9120-515c-8370-2f1d8ca0a88e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553412}] :question/id #uuid "c0707c67-e191-5de9-a1ec-b2b0c2ac11dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-70107" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-4y=7 \\\\ 5x-2y=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-4y=7 \\\\ 5x-2y=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing an Unknown in Terms of 2x2 Determinants"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119601 :question/id #uuid "311ba12f-de22-580c-9e0d-6766f7864d1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4578" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,3 \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y &=4 \\\\ x+ky &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. ![](images/7d/7d767f03d04f1c35a774ef089d706894b2fe426995f1c7c050f8c5af0ef9e1e2.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/5a/5a9fe2dde8c8202f4532f745f0fac58ec649bdf39e928560ccaa2485d0f6f09e.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/7e/7e2a9ca703f25107de026c84a8e7feb6099b48c7794ffdc166108358701feb8f.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\dfrac{D_y}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{36}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}| & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2cccd7a-59dd-5341-8da8-c4f9f2d79f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459999} {:answer/id #uuid "04a392a3-420f-5a31-8d4f-8bae69f8b393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459999} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0190c3-07d8-5ac7-a1e8-147955249f22" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460866} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "791f8129-8699-58f1-a9a9-b6ebcd9c6173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460001} {:answer/id #uuid "0bf4cc1c-cd15-55c4-ad6d-fa2f525a3ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460002} :answer-field/id #uuid "75497c77-e3e0-5972-9d79-4905d1203258" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cbcb43-3872-5c6f-8951-67efc30c2272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460003} {:answer/id #uuid "f34324fc-c44c-5284-a295-52655ea09cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460003} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f6b517a-4f4f-5395-9950-bac2def4f360" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460868}] :question/id #uuid "14b8a882-c4c9-5cd4-86d8-6c02afbea2c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42612" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-y &=k \\\\ -2x+4y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n- **I.** $|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|=-6$\n- **II.** $|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|=-54$\n- **III.** $|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|=18$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20-2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|}{18} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}| & =-54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba1160fd-512a-55ca-a11f-396ebf95e85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477319} {:answer/id #uuid "0e095f2f-a440-5ea9-8e28-b0f802fe77ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477320}] :answer-field/corre