" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402855}] :question/id #uuid "d34758b2-12b1-52b8-b1d1-a0b7cc3976d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-42982" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+9y &=\\,0 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,x+2y &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as  $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+9y &=\\,0 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,x+2y &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\,\\text{ }0 & 9 \\\\ \\,-1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249e501c-2f96-5d3a-aca1-a06ed673e1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 5 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549393} {:answer/id #uuid "2592d63c-e1be-5dbf-bf09-9ff0d9f2f561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,11 & 8 \\\\ \\,-28 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549394} {:answer/id #uuid "41433e06-ff80-564d-9ce3-d0dc9852a8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549448} {:answer/id #uuid "752e27ae-6bc8-5acc-bb2a-b47134fc80d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549538} {:answer/id #uuid "8a477f06-b877-5fa0-bce3-9f5744ae6971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & \\,-28 \\\\ 2 & \\,11\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549538} :answer-field/id #uuid "8429c5f3-10e5-5e89-82ae-2a624d986bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549858}] :question/id #uuid "fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42981" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b1b1aa-24d1-5aef-a813-7fc53de93a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}24 & -5 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553161} {:answer/id #uuid "96f462da-d01b-534e-a1e3-fd347851fcb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}24 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553176} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b7b301-56fa-5684-a9f3-0ab3079d524c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}24 & -13 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553265} {:answer/id #uuid "e976bed0-1a5a-5653-a411-e8f6c2bf8b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 26 & -5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553332} {:answer/id #uuid "f48c2eb6-30d8-58ef-9af2-4c4aeb973c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-13 & 24 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}24 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553176} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa95dfd1-6be9-59ed-a133-250089e63006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553458}] :question/id #uuid "5c559d0c-7ff0-552e-86fe-3f0db4a6f01e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42604" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-13x-6y=24 \\\\ \\,8x-5y=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as \n$x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}-13 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-13x-6y=24 \\\\ \\,8x-5y=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}-13 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}24 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parts of Cramer's Rule Formula"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1775 :topic/title "Cramer's Rule for 2x2 Systems of Linear Equations"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120720} :activity/id #uuid "87481402-fa01-553f-b61a-d34f3d67f347" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121053 :tutorial/content "Consider the following equations of a line $l$ and a plane $\\Pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nl: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2,2,1 \\rangle + t \\langle 3,0,1 \\rangle, \\qquad \\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 3,-5,1 \\rangle = 7.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the intersection point $P$ of $l$ and $\\Pi?$\n\nWe start by writing the equation of the line in the form $\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2+3t , 2, 1+t \\rangle.$ Now, since $P$ must lie on the line $l,$ we can write its coordinates as $P(2+3t , 2, 1+t)$ for some particular value of $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n![](images/d3/d3ba164eb98a53c463744e11d6593177ddde2a0a309f752e5d86b30db0d79925.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ also lies on the plane $\\Pi.$ So, the coordinates $P(2+3t , 2, 1+t)$ must satisfy the equation of the plane as well. Substituting the position vector $\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2+3t , 2, 1+t \\rangle$ into the equation of the plane and solving for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,1⟩ & =7 \\\\ ⟨2+3t,2,1+t⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3(2+3t)-5\\cdot 2+(1+t) & =7 \\\\ 6+9t-10+1+t & =7 \\\\ 10t & =10 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨2+3(1),2,1+(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(5, 2, 2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2ca8cb0-0b92-5b99-ac8b-edd2f2a6dbb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3259 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a814365d-ddd3-5356-ac6d-30d046db60e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abef3da6-252e-51e4-b816-9ef7233b9a5d"} :step/id #uuid "ddf088ee-3776-5bfe-8145-83247b42b9d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120723}} {:db/id #edb/r